  "notes": "Pass 2 on the same estate of 115,535 entities, scored on the CPU the hub and Postgres spend, because other work on the machine made wall time swing by a third.\n\n- **A batch's relations are looked up by entity, and the source is matched in code.** The query used to ask for one source's relations. When Postgres's statistics were stale, as they are while a fresh table fills, it walked every relation the source ever wrote, once per entity in the batch: 7.6 s for one lookup, 15 ms now. This is why the first push swung from 5 s to 54 s.\n- **A rule that matches on a stored value reads stored values alone.** It used to build both blueprints whole, derived fields and status included, and again after every rule that changed links. Re-linking after the second push fell from 21 s to under 1 s.\n- **An entity's page and a walk copy what a blueprint already keeps.** They used to derive the entity and everything its derived fields read. A page fell from about 330 ms to 24 ms, a two-hop walk from 900 ms to 13 ms.\n- **A blueprint's build borrows what other blueprints keep.** Deriving one blueprint loads the neighbours its derived fields read, and theirs. Now a neighbour another blueprint already keeps stands in, and the blueprints others read are built first. The summary and search halved. A walk inside a re-link still reads afresh.\n- **Inheritance says what it changed as it changes it.** It used to say so once at the end of a pass, and named only blueprints it had never changed before, so a second change to one blueprint refreshed nothing.\n\nTried and not kept:\n\n- Writing a rule's links in one statement each way: linking after the first push halved, but the rest of the run moved more than it saved.\n- Cheaper derivation per entity (narrow relation reads, one source lookup per pass, no list copies): 4.3 % better, inside this pass's noise floor of 10.9 %, which one slow baseline run set. It goes into the next pass."
}
